E1 WHAT IS WRONG TODAY (LinkedIn editor and Links tab)

EP-1 [Container] The editor expands inline inside a 2px black frame with a second card inside it: two borders and double padding on a 390px screen, the list jumps, and one block takes about 2.5 phone screens.
EP-2 [Order] The preview - the thing being designed - sits at the very bottom, below every control, and Save/Cancel are below that, so the owner scrolls back and forth.
EP-3 [Source] The source is set in two ways at once: a large blue "Reconnect LinkedIn" button with an "Account connected" chip, and "Or paste LinkedIn link" with a detected-type check. The loudest button is the rarest action.
EP-4 [Copy] Owner-facing status text reaches the public card: the preview body says "Connected LinkedIn profile" and the button says "Connect" (the placeholder of "Button text"). The title says "embed" (reserved for Share-sheet widgets) and the helper says "OAuth".
EP-5 [Looks] Five LinkedIn-specific looks ("Executive Blue", "Cover Banner", "Frosted Network", "Compact Business", "Match Page Vibe"), each with a two-line description; every other platform presumably invents its own list. The mini thumbnails are near-identical blue bars, so they do not show what differs. The selected state is a thick outline with a tiny corner check.
EP-6 [Controls] Checkboxes for "Show bio / summary" and "Show stats" instead of switches, shown even where the platform has no stats (LinkedIn has no public stats); "Button text" has only a placeholder.
EP-7 [Labels] "Card title / caption", "Card look & style", "LinkedIn Preset", "Live look preview" and an uppercase "LINKEDIN" banner label: Title Case, tracked caps, and several names for the same two things.
EP-8 [List] The filter chips are cut off at the right edge ("Archived (1..."). "Media" mixes profile cards (LinkedIn, GitHub, X) with playable media (YouTube). Row titles and subtitles are cut ("Arjun Bharti ...", "youtube.com/...", "github.com/Arj...") so similar rows are hard to tell apart.
EP-9 [Many editors] The same editor is presumably repeated for YouTube, X, GitHub, Spotify, Instagram, Facebook, Threads, Discord and Linktree with small differences; each difference is accidental complexity and a maintenance cost.
EP-10 [Connections] "Reconnect LinkedIn" means connections are managed inside each editor; there is no single place to see or revoke them (the Account page shows none).


E2 ONE EDITOR FOR EVERY PLATFORM BLOCK

ED-1 SMART ADD. In the block picker the first tile is "Paste a link". Pasting any URL detects the provider (YouTube, X, GitHub, LinkedIn, Instagram, Facebook, Threads, Discord, Linktree, Spotify, Pinterest, SoundCloud, or generic) and the kind (profile, video, playlist, track, board, post ...), creates the matching block with that provider's defaults, and opens the editor with the live preview already showing real data. An unknown URL becomes a plain Link (BUTTON shell). The per-provider tiles stay in the picker under "Social" and "Players" for people who browse. Detection is one pure function with unit tests covering www., m. and mobile hosts, short links (youtu.be, spotify.link, pin.it, lnkd.in, discord.gg) and tracking parameters.

ED-2 ONE EDITOR, ONE DEFINITION. Every platform block opens the same editor component with the same anatomy, order and wording. The provider definition (PV-1) only decides which fields and toggles appear. No per-provider editor screens. Adding a provider needs a definition file and an icon, nothing else.

ED-3 CONTAINER. Phones: a full-height bottom sheet (drag handle, close button). Desktop: a dialog with two columns, form on the left and live preview on the right (same pattern as the Share sheet, master SH-1). Never expand inline in the list and never add an outer frame. The row being edited keeps its place in the list with the active state from the shared rules. Title: "Edit {Platform} card" or "Edit {Platform} player" (never "embed").

ED-4 SHEET LAYOUT, top to bottom: header (icon, title, close) -> pinned live preview -> Source -> Content -> Look (or Size for players) -> More options (collapsed) -> sticky footer. The preview is pinned at the top while the form scrolls (about 30% of the viewport, 160-260px, with a small handle to collapse it); on desktop it is the sticky right column. It is the real renderer with real data (RU-5, RU-6) and updates 150ms after every change and when the source resolves.

ED-5 SOURCE. One field labelled "Link" with a provider-specific placeholder (for example "linkedin.com/in/yourname"). On success a small chip names what was detected ("LinkedIn profile", "YouTube channel", "Spotify playlist"); on failure an inline message ("This doesn't look like a LinkedIn link"). While resolving show a spinner, then fill Title and the preview. If resolving fails, the owner can continue with manual details (ED-13) and sees "We couldn't load details from {Platform}. Your card will use what you enter below." For providers that support a connection, a compact row sits under the field: a "Connected" chip with a small menu (Reconnect, Disconnect) when connected, or a tertiary "Connect account" button when not; connecting returns to the editor with its state intact. The large blue "Reconnect" button is removed. Copy never says "OAuth" ("Connect your account to show verified details and live numbers."). Listing and revoking connections lives only in Account > Connected platforms (AR-3); the editor only starts the flow and shows status.

ED-6 DISPLAY AS. Where a provider supports both, a segmented control "Card | Player" (for example Spotify, YouTube videos, Pinterest, Instagram/Facebook/Threads posts). Changing it swaps the preview. Providers with one mode hide the control. Registry defaults (PV-1): players for videos, audio and posts; cards for profiles.

ED-7 CONTENT (always visible). "Title" (prefilled from the source, 60 characters, replaces "Card title / caption"), a "Show summary" switch, a "Show stats" switch and "Button text" (placeholder = the provider default from PV-3, 24 characters; empty uses the default). Toggles appear only when the provider can supply that data (capabilities in PV-1); if it cannot, the toggle is hidden, not disabled.

ED-8 LOOK. For cards: five look tiles in a 2-column grid (names and meaning in LK-1). Each tile draws its own silhouette in the current brand colour (LK-13), so differences are visible. Selected = ring + check. The one-line description of the selected look appears under the grid, not inside every tile. A tertiary link "Use page default" clears a block-level override. For players this section becomes "Size" (for example Compact | Standard | Large) where the provider offers sizes, and is hidden where it does not.

ED-9 MORE OPTIONS (collapsed; the header summarises non-default values, for example "Brand accent, Pill corners"): Accent (Brand | Page | Custom colour), Corners (Page | Sharp | Rounded | Pill), "Show avatar" (artwork for items), "Show platform label", "Button style" (Filled | Outline | Text | Hidden), and "Override details" (ED-13). A tertiary "Reset to defaults" restores them.

ED-10 FOOTER. Sticky: primary "Save changes", tertiary "Cancel". Save is disabled until something changes, shows a spinner, then closes the sheet with a "Saved" toast and updates the row. Cancel with unsaved changes asks to discard. Delete, archive and duplicate stay in the row's "..." menu, not in the editor.

ED-11 CONTROL VOCABULARY. Follow section 0.5: switches (never checkboxes), segmented controls for 2-4 options, tiles for looks, sentence case, no uppercase tracked labels, no truncated labels.

ED-12 STATES. Empty (no link yet): the preview shows a neutral skeleton with "Paste a link to see your card". Resolving: spinner. Error: inline message plus manual details. Connection expired: warning chip "Needs reconnecting" plus a Reconnect button. Rate limited or provider down: keep the last snapshot and show "Showing saved details". Private or unsupported source: "This profile is private. Add details yourself below."

ED-13 MANUAL DETAILS ("Override details", inside More options, opened automatically when a source cannot be resolved): Name, Handle, Image (upload), Summary (160 characters). A filled field overrides the resolved value; an empty field uses it. For providers without a usable data source (Instagram, Facebook and Threads profiles, Linktree, Pinterest profiles, LinkedIn headline) these fields are the normal way to fill the card.

ED-14 LIST ROWS AND FILTERS (extends master L-4 and L-5). Chips wrap onto several lines (no clipping) and show only groups that have items: All, Links, Social (profile and item cards), Players, Support, Forms, Headers, Archived. "Media" is retired. A row shows: drag handle, icon tile (brand icon), title with a type badge holding the provider name, a subtitle (handle, or domain and path; an ellipsis only at the end), the visibility switch and the "..." menu. A small "needs attention" icon (disconnected, source failing for 3 refreshes) appears on the row with a tooltip and opens the editor. Archive stays reversible and archived blocks never render publicly.


E3 CARD LOOKS AND STYLING OPTIONS

LK-1 FIVE LOOKS, THE SAME FOR EVERY PROVIDER (names and one-line meanings; legacy preset names keep working through an alias table).
1. Match page - "Uses your page colors and button style." The default. Legacy: "Match Page Vibe".
2. Brand - "Clean card with the platform's color on the icon and button." Legacy: "Executive Blue" and the classic or corporate preset of any other platform.
3. Banner - "Colored banner with the avatar overlapping it." Legacy: "Cover Banner".
4. Glass - "Frosted card that lets your background show through." Legacy: "Frosted Network".
5. Compact - "One slim row: picture, name and button." Legacy: "Compact Business".
Other platforms' existing preset names map to the closest of the five by purpose (read the preset lists in the code). The mapping is applied when reading; stored values are never rewritten; an unknown value falls back to Match page.

LK-2 ANATOMY AND SLOTS (shared by every look). Slots: banner (optional) - picture (avatar for profile cards, artwork for item cards) - name and subtitle - summary - stats - button - platform glyph. A look only changes arrangement, surface and accent; a provider only supplies data and never layout. Two content kinds: profile cards (avatar-led: LinkedIn, X, GitHub, Instagram, Facebook, Threads, YouTube channel, Linktree, Discord server, Pinterest profile) and item cards (artwork-led: Spotify items, YouTube videos and playlists, Pinterest pins and boards).

LK-3 MEASUREMENTS (phone column, 328-640px). Card padding 16px; 8px between lines in a group, 12px between groups. Picture: avatar 48px circle (Banner 56px, Compact 40px); item artwork 64px square (Compact 48px) with an 8px radius. Text: name 16/22 semibold; subtitle 14/20 muted; summary 14/20 in the secondary colour, two lines at most; stats 13/18 muted; platform glyph 16px. Button: 36px high (Compact 32px), label 13/16 semibold, radius from the Corners token. The whole card is one link (master PG-17); minimum height 64px (Compact 64px, others grow with content).

LK-4 MATCH PAGE. SURFACE shell exactly as master PG-17 and PG-20: --surface, --border, the page's corner radius (capped at 28px), a monochrome platform glyph at the top right, summary, stats on the left and the button on the right. Default button style: Text (14/20 semibold plus an external-link icon). No platform colour anywhere.

LK-5 BRAND. Same SURFACE shell and layout as Match page, plus the platform's brand colour: a 1px border at 24% brand colour over --surface (the boundary must still reach 3:1 against the page background, otherwise use --border-strong), the platform glyph in the brand colour, and a filled button in the brand colour with automatic black or white text (at least 4.5:1). If the brand colour reaches less than 3:1 against --surface (for example black on a dark vibe) use --text as the accent instead; the accent is never invisible. Default button style: Filled.

LK-6 BANNER. A 72px banner across the top of the card, clipped by the card radius. Profile cards: a gradient from the brand colour to a darker shade of it. Item cards with a wide image (YouTube thumbnail, Pinterest pin): the image, centre-cropped, under a 30% black scrim. In the banner: the platform glyph and platform name in sentence case (no uppercase), white, at the top left over a 40% black scrim strip for contrast. The avatar (56px) overlaps the banner's bottom edge by 28px with a 3px ring in --surface. Name, subtitle, summary, stats and the button sit below. Default button style: Filled. Provider restrictions override this (LK-12): artwork that must stay unmodified is never used as a banner.

LK-7 GLASS. The surface is --surface at 60% opacity over the page background with a 16px backdrop blur and a 1px light border (white at 28%, or --border on light vibes), plus a 6-8% brand tint. Text must stay at 4.5:1 against the worst case of the page background (use the scrim logic of master PG-7); if the page background could break that, raise the surface opacity to 85% automatically. Without backdrop-filter support the surface is opaque. The editor shows a hint when the page background is a plain colour: "Glass looks best over a photo or gradient background." Default button style: Outline.

LK-8 COMPACT. One row, 64px high: picture 40px (artwork 48px) | name over one line of summary or handle | a small button (32px; icon-only arrow under 340px). No stats row; with "Show stats" on, append the first stat to the subtitle ("12 followers"). Default button style: Filled.

LK-9 PAGE DEFAULT (Appearance > Cards, master AP-1 order). A "Cards" accordion section with the same five look tiles as ED-8 and the helper "Individual cards can override this." It applies to every card without its own override. Corners follow Appearance > Buttons; there is no separate corners control here. A block's look is inherited by default; choosing a tile sets an override; "Use page default" clears it. Resolution order: block override, page default, Match page.

LK-10 ACCENT AND BRAND COLOURS. Accent options: Brand (the provider's colour), Page (--accent) or Custom (the owner's colour). Every accent passes the same contrast guard (button text at least 4.5:1, glyph and border at least 3:1 against --surface; otherwise fall back to --text). Default brand colours (confirm against each platform's brand page): LinkedIn #0A66C2; X #000000; YouTube #FF0000; GitHub #181717; Spotify - Spotify green from Spotify's brand page (commonly #1DB954, with black text on it); Instagram #E4405F (no gradients); Facebook #1877F2; Threads #000000; Discord #5865F2; Linktree #43E660 (black text); Pinterest #E60023; SoundCloud #FF5500.

LK-11 THE COMPLETE OPTION LIST (nothing else is exposed). Look (5), Accent (3), Corners (Page, Sharp, Rounded, Pill), Show avatar (artwork for items), Show summary, Show stats, Show platform label, Button text, Button style (Filled, Outline, Text, Hidden), Display as (Card, Player), Size (players), Override details (name, handle, summary, image). No free-form CSS, per-card fonts, shadows or sizes. Cards inherit the page's fonts and vibe tokens. Store the choices as one presentation-only settings object on the block, for example { look, accent, accentColor, corners, show: { avatar, summary, stats, label }, buttonText, buttonStyle, displayAs, size, override: { name, handle, summary, image } }, with sensible defaults when a key is missing (old blocks keep working).

LK-12 FIT RULES AND PROVIDER RESTRICTIONS. Every look must render correctly with: a 60-character name (two lines, then ellipsis), a missing avatar (fallback tile with the platform glyph), a missing summary (the row disappears), zero or unavailable stats (hidden), missing artwork (a glyph tile), right-to-left text (dir="auto"), and a 320px screen. A provider definition can restrict looks through flags: for example Spotify requires unmodified artwork, the Spotify logo and a link back (PV-8); the renderer obeys the flags, and the editor hides or disables options that would break them with a reason ("Spotify artwork can't be recoloured").

LK-13 LOOK TILES. In the editor and in Appearance > Cards each tile draws its own silhouette with the current tokens and the provider's brand colour: Match page = surface with two text bars and a text-style button; Brand = surface with a brand glyph and a filled brand button; Banner = a coloured band with an overlapping circle; Glass = a translucent card over a gradient; Compact = one slim row. Tiles are 4:3, the name sits under the drawing, the selected tile has the ring and check. Tiles are drawn from the real renderer at small scale when that is cheap; otherwise from the silhouettes. They must never all look the same.


E4 PROVIDERS, DATA PIPELINE AND CREDENTIALS

PV-1 PROVIDER REGISTRY. One definition per provider in one folder: id, name, brand colour, icon, URL patterns mapped to kinds, per-kind capabilities (avatar, artwork, banner image, name, handle, summary, list of stats, player), the resolution strategies in order (PV-2), default button text per kind (PV-3), allowed display modes and the default, player sizes, connection support (yes or no, scopes), refresh interval (PV-4), brand and terms flags (for example Spotify: artwork stays unmodified, logo required, link back required) and the link-field placeholder per kind. The editor, the renderer, the detector and the refresh job all read the registry; there is no provider-specific code anywhere else.

PV-2 RESOLUTION STRATEGIES, in order; the first that works wins; every result is stored as a snapshot with its source and time. (1) The provider's official API with server-side credentials, when available and allowed. (2) The provider's oEmbed endpoint (no credentials). (3) Open Graph unfurl of the public page (title, image, description; public pages only, honour robots.txt and the provider's terms, 3 second timeout, 1 MB cap). (4) The owner's manual details (ED-13). Visitors' browsers never call third-party APIs (master PG-36). A resolver returns one shape: kind, title, subtitle, avatar, artwork, banner, summary, stats (list of key and value), embed (src plus aspect or height plus allowed attributes), canonicalUrl, brand colour, fetchedAt, source.

PV-3 DEFAULT BUTTON TEXT (the owner can change it; never owner-facing status text on a public card). LinkedIn "View profile"; X "Follow"; YouTube channel "Subscribe", video and playlist "Watch"; GitHub profile "View profile", repo "View repo"; Instagram, Threads and Facebook "Follow" (posts "View post"); Discord "Join server"; Linktree "Open"; Spotify "Listen on Spotify"; Pinterest pin "View pin", board "View board", profile "Follow"; generic "Open". Strings are translatable.

PV-4 SNAPSHOTS AND REFRESH (extends master PG-36). Store one snapshot per block (the PV-2 shape) and render it on the server. Refresh profile stats every 6 hours and profile details and media metadata every 24 hours, with jitter; the row menu has "Refresh" (once a minute per block). A failure keeps the last snapshot; after 3 consecutive failures set "needs attention" (ED-14). Copy images (avatars, artwork) to iveo storage unless the provider's rules say otherwise: Spotify artwork and Pinterest widgets stay unmodified and are shown as the provider supplies them (PV-8, PV-9), so the renderer loads the provider's image URL with explicit width and height.

PV-5 URLS AND SAFETY. Normalise links (lower-case host, strip tracking parameters such as si, utm_*, igshid, fbclid). Expand short links on the server (at most 3 redirects, http and https only, ports 80 and 443, block private and local addresses to prevent SSRF, 3 second timeout, 1 MB cap). Accept only http, https, mailto and tel (master PG-39). Render all provider text as text, never as HTML. Never insert an oEmbed "html" field raw: use only the iframe src and the attributes the provider definition allows.

PV-6 SECRETS. Credentials live only in server environment variables and are read through one server-only configuration module that fails at boot in production when a required one is missing. Names (values stay empty in the repository):
SPOTIFY_CLIENT_ID, SPOTIFY_CLIENT_SECRET
PINTEREST_APP_ID, PINTEREST_ACCESS_TOKEN, PINTEREST_APP_SECRET (only when available), PINTEREST_OAUTH_ENABLED (default false)
YOUTUBE_API_KEY (only if the YouTube Data API is used), GITHUB_TOKEN (optional, read-only public access)
plus the existing LinkedIn and X variables, if any.
Rules: no public prefix (nothing the framework exposes to the browser); never committed; never logged (mask in logs and errors); never returned by an API or placed in a client bundle; never used in tests (use mocks); an `.env.example` with empty values; secret scanning in CI. The values pasted into the chat must be rotated before use (see the security notice). A Pinterest app id is an identifier rather than a secret, but it also belongs in the environment, not in code.

PV-7 OTHER PROVIDERS (defaults; re-verify each provider's current documentation before relying on an endpoint, RU-10).
- YouTube - channel, video, playlist. Channel card: avatar, name, handle, subscriber and video counts when public, from the YouTube Data API v3 with a server-side key (cached 24 hours, quota-aware); fallback oEmbed (title, thumbnail), then manual. Player: youtube-nocookie.com embed, 16:9, lazy. No connection needed.
- X - profile, post. Card: keep today's data source (check the code) and show only the stats it can supply. Post: X's official embed only as a Player, loaded on interaction because it needs a heavy third-party script.
- GitHub - user or organisation, repo. Card from the public REST API (user: name, login, avatar, bio, public repos, followers; repo: description, stars, forks, main language) with an optional read-only server token (GITHUB_TOKEN) to avoid the unauthenticated rate limit; cached 6 hours.
- LinkedIn - profile, company. There is no public profile API for ordinary apps: the card uses the owner's connection (name and photo of the connecting user) plus a manual headline; no stats; no scraping.
- Instagram - profile, post, reel. Profile card = manual details plus the link (no stats unless a supported source exists). Posts and reels: Meta's oEmbed as a Player. Meta opened its oEmbed endpoints for public Facebook, Threads and Instagram content without an access token in June 2026; rate limits may be lower than token-based access, and oEmbed returns embed HTML for posts and videos, not profile data.
- Facebook - page, profile, post, video. Page and profile cards = manual details plus link. Posts and videos: Meta oEmbed Player. The Page Plugin iframe is an optional Player for pages.
- Threads - profile, post. Profile card = manual details plus link. Posts: Meta oEmbed Player.
- Discord - server invite. Card from Discord's public invite lookup with counts (server name, icon, approximate member and online numbers) - verify the current endpoint and limits. An expired or invalid invite sets "needs attention" and keeps the last snapshot.
- Linktree - page. No API: Open Graph unfurl (title, image, description) or manual details.
- SoundCloud - the existing player; oEmbed for title and artwork.
